ities plan identifies Great Space as the core of the Sayles-Hill Campus Center and describes it as welcoming students, faculty, and staff.","source_url":"https://cdn.carleton.edu/uploads/sites/221/2019/10/FacilitiesMasterPlan_May2014.pdf","source_title":"Carleton College Facilities Master Plan","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":"2014-05-01","source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Campus-center entry and individual services may follow current college access rules and operating schedules.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-bald-spot","subject_type":"place","entity_name":"Bald Spot","title":"Take Reading Outside","subtitle":"The central lawn beside Sayles-Hill","notes":"Bring a book or Frisbee when conditions are dry; the open lawn works for an outdoor study break or informal recreation.","short_summary":"The central lawn supports both quiet reading and casual student activity.","evidence_claim":"Carleton’s official image archive documents students studying on the Bald Spot in fall 2023.","source_url":"https://imagencloud.carleton.edu/record/67495","source_title":"Bald Spot Fall 2023","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":"2023-10-16","source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Outdoor use depends on weather, grounds conditions, and any scheduled campus activity.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-recreation-center","subject_type":"place","entity_name":"Recreation Center","title":"Choose Your Own Kind of Movement","subtitle":"Courts, track, climbing, and fitness under one roof","notes":"Check the recreation schedule, then choose an open-use court, the indoor track, a fitness area, or an appropriate climbing option.","short_summary":"One facility accommodates several forms of individual and group recreation.","evidence_claim":"Carleton Athletics states that the Recreation Center supports recreation including basketball, tennis, weightlifting, rock climbing, aerobics, and racquet sports.","source_url":"https://athletics.carleton.edu/sports/2019/7/15/recreation-center.aspx?id=6006","source_title":"Recreation Center","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Confirm current building hours, eligibility, reservations, supervision, and equipment requirements before participating.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-upper-arboretum","subject_type":"place","entity_name":"Upper Arboretum","title":"Pick a Trail That Fits the Day","subtitle":"The campus-side section of Cowling Arboretum","notes":"Consult the trail map before walking, running, skiing, or biking, and keep bicycles only on designated Upper Arb routes.","short_summary":"The Upper Arb offers a denser trail network close to campus for several kinds of recreation.","evidence_claim":"Carleton’s Arboretum trail map says visitors use its trails for hiking, running, limited biking, cross-country skiing, and nature appreciation.","source_url":"https://cdn.carleton.edu/uploads/sites/594/2020/12/2017B_WArbMapWithRules.pdf","source_title":"Cowling Arboretum Trail Map and Visitor Rules","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Stay on trails, leash dogs, do not camp, and observe seasonal archery-hunt restrictions and posted rules.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-dacie-moses-house","subject_type":"place","entity_name":"Dacie Moses House","title":"Bake in a House Built Around Hospitality","subtitle":"A small campus house with a student baking culture","notes":"Check the house’s current access guidance before visiting, then leave the kitchen and shared rooms ready for the next group.","short_summary":"A domestic-scale campus space has long brought students together through baking, music, conversation, and study breaks.","evidence_claim":"A Carleton-hosted study of Dacie Moses House documents students baking cookies and muffins, studying, playing music, talking, and gathering there.","source_url":"https://cdn.carleton.edu/uploads/sites/275/2020/01/Sherri_sPaper.pdf","source_title":"A Study of Dacie Moses House","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Historical descriptions do not establish present-day unrestricted access; verify current house guidance before entering or using the kitchen.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-daugherty-atrium","subject_type":"study_space","entity_name":"Daugherty Atrium","title":"Work Where the Sciences Intersect","subtitle":"The central shared space in Evelyn M. Anderson Hall","notes":"Use an available workspace between classes, and keep circulation routes clear around the atrium’s connecting stairs and ramps.","short_summary":"The science complex’s central atrium combines circulation, student work areas, and public academic exchange.","evidence_claim":"Carleton’s campus-history project says the Anderson Hall atrium connects three science buildings and contains multiple student workspaces.","source_url":"https://campushistory.sites.carleton.edu/science-essay/","source_title":"A History of Science Facilities at Carleton","source_kind":"university_official","source_published_at":null,"source_fetched_at":"2026-08-13","latitude":null,"longitude":null,"maps_url":null,"access_notes":"Use is subject to current building access, class schedules, posted events, and any reserved portions of the atrium.","cost_status":"unknown","under21_safe":true},{"subject_id":"carleton-goodsell-ents-lounge","subject_type":"study_space","entity_name":"Goodsell Observato